1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,097
In the heart of the Pentagon, a
government official named Bob Taylor sat
in

2
00:00:05,097 --> 00:00:09,020
his office, staring at three massive,
clunky computer terminals.

3
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:13,466
Each of these large machines was wired
to a completely different giant

4
00:00:13,466 --> 00:00:15,132
mainframe computer far away.

5
00:00:15,412 --> 00:00:20,172
Was this messy office setup the actual
birthplace of the modern Internet, or

6
00:00:20,172 --> 00:00:24,556
was our digital world forged as a shield
against nuclear annihilation?

7
00:00:24,836 --> 00:00:29,965
The year was 1966, and Taylor was
managing research funding for the
Advanced

8
00:00:29,965 --> 00:00:31,652
Research Projects Agency.

9
00:00:31,932 --> 00:00:36,240
To communicate with his university
researchers across the country, he had
to

10
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:37,884
grapple with a bizarre setup.

11
00:00:38,164 --> 00:00:42,509
A terminal in his office linked directly
to the System Development

12
00:00:42,509 --> 00:00:45,340
Corporation Q-32 mainframe in Santa
Monica.

13
00:00:45,620 --> 00:00:49,076
A second terminal connected to Project
Genie at Berkeley.

14
00:00:49,356 --> 00:00:53,364
The last terminal communicated with the
Multics system at MIT.

15
00:00:53,644 --> 00:00:57,597
Whenever Taylor wanted to talk to
different researchers, his frustration

16
00:00:57,597 --> 00:00:58,036
mounted.

17
00:00:58,316 --> 00:01:02,293
He had to physically stand up, walk over
to another desk, and sit in a

18
00:01:02,293 --> 00:01:04,964
different chair just to use a separate
machine.

19
00:01:05,244 --> 00:01:09,561
Even worse, each computer required a
completely different set of user

20
00:01:09,561 --> 00:01:10,812
commands to operate.

21
00:01:11,092 --> 00:01:15,161
He could not easily share information
between these academic researchers

22
00:01:15,161 --> 00:01:18,100
because their systems could not speak to
each other.

23
00:01:18,380 --> 00:01:23,492
Shuffling between his chairs, Taylor had
a simple yet powerful realization.

24
00:01:23,772 --> 00:01:26,700
The clutter in his office was highly
inefficient.

25
00:01:26,980 --> 00:01:30,460
There should not be multiple terminals
for multiple computers.

26
00:01:30,740 --> 00:01:35,302
Instead, there ought to be a single
network that could connect any terminal

27
00:01:35,302 --> 00:01:36,884
to any computer, anywhere.

28
00:01:37,164 --> 00:01:41,138
This simple desire to clean up his
office and connect his researchers

29
00:01:41,138 --> 00:01:43,788
launched a project that would change
humanity.

30
00:01:44,068 --> 00:01:48,370
But how did this office struggle turn
into the global network we use today,

31
00:01:48,370 --> 00:01:52,156
and where did the famous myth of a
nuclear-proof shield come from?

32
00:01:53,056 --> 00:01:57,527
Before Bob Taylor ever paced between
those three terminals in his Pentagon

33
00:01:57,527 --> 00:02:01,816
office, another thinker had already
dreamed of a unified digital world.

34
00:02:02,096 --> 00:02:06,475
This man was Joseph Carl Robnett
Licklider, a psychologist and computer

35
00:02:06,475 --> 00:02:08,696
scientist who went by the name Lick.

36
00:02:08,976 --> 00:02:13,855
Born in St. Louis, Missouri, he was
fascinated by how humans and machines

37
00:02:13,855 --> 00:02:15,192
could work together.

38
00:02:15,472 --> 00:02:20,156
During the Cold War, he worked as a
human factors expert on a project called

39
00:02:20,156 --> 00:02:22,744
SAGE, a computer-aided air defense
system.

40
00:02:23,024 --> 00:02:27,634
SAGE collected and presented radar data
to human operators, who then chose

41
00:02:27,634 --> 00:02:29,192
the appropriate response.

42
00:02:29,472 --> 00:02:33,980
This project convinced Licklider of the
massive potential of interactive

43
00:02:33,980 --> 00:02:35,232
computer interfaces.

44
00:02:35,512 --> 00:02:39,758
He published a paper on man-computer
symbiosis, describing a partnership

45
00:02:39,758 --> 00:02:43,885
where machines would handle routine
tasks to prepare the way for human

46
00:02:43,885 --> 00:02:44,416
insights.

47
00:02:44,696 --> 00:02:48,941
After learning about time-sharing in
Paris, Licklider joined the firm Bolt

48
00:02:48,941 --> 00:02:53,070
Beranek and Newman, where he conducted
the first public demonstration of

49
00:02:53,070 --> 00:02:53,816
time-sharing.

50
00:02:54,096 --> 00:02:59,352
In April 1963, he wrote several
memoranda to his colleagues that
outlined a

51
00:02:59,352 --> 00:03:01,104
highly ambitious concept.

52
00:03:01,384 --> 00:03:04,768
He called his vision the Intergalactic
Computer Network.

53
00:03:05,048 --> 00:03:07,448
He did not mean a network for outer
space.

54
00:03:07,728 --> 00:03:12,034
Instead, he imagined a global network
that allowed users to share

55
00:03:12,034 --> 00:03:16,008
information and access programs from any
computer, anywhere.

56
00:03:16,288 --> 00:03:20,789
He foresaw a future of online libraries,
electronic commerce, and software

57
00:03:20,789 --> 00:03:24,256
that would migrate wherever it was
needed on the network.

58
00:03:24,536 --> 00:03:29,126
To turn this dream into reality,
Licklider needed a platform with

59
00:03:29,126 --> 00:03:30,680
significant resources.

60
00:03:30,960 --> 00:03:35,678
He found it in October 1963, when he
became the head of the Behavioral

61
00:03:35,678 --> 00:03:39,048
Sciences and Command and Control
programs at ARPA.

62
00:03:39,328 --> 00:03:43,400
In this role, he began directing
government funds toward time-sharing

63
00:03:43,400 --> 00:03:45,112
research at top universities.

64
00:03:45,392 --> 00:03:48,896
Licklider was exceptionally good at
sharing his enthusiasm.

65
00:03:49,176 --> 00:03:53,476
He successfully convinced his
successors, Ivan Sutherland and Bob
Taylor,

66
00:03:53,476 --> 00:03:57,895
that a cooperative computer network was
an important goal for the future of

67
00:03:57,895 --> 00:03:58,896
computer science.

68
00:03:59,176 --> 00:04:03,796
He argued that computers should not just
do calculations, but should help

69
00:04:03,796 --> 00:04:05,632
humans think and collaborate.

70
00:04:05,912 --> 00:04:10,786
Licklider left ARPA before any actual
development contracts for the network

71
00:04:10,786 --> 00:04:11,696
were assigned.

72
00:04:11,976 --> 00:04:16,392
Even so, his ideas remained the guiding
light for the agency.

73
00:04:16,672 --> 00:04:18,544
He had set the target.

74
00:04:18,824 --> 00:04:23,315
When Taylor eventually stared at his
office terminals, he was ready to make

75
00:04:23,315 --> 00:04:24,992
Licklider's dream a reality.

76
00:04:25,892 --> 00:04:30,368
Taylor wanted to share academic
resources, but a completely different,

77
00:04:30,368 --> 00:04:34,076
darker worry was brewing in the halls of
military defense.

78
00:04:34,356 --> 00:04:38,679
During the height of the Cold War, the
military feared that a nuclear strike

79
00:04:38,679 --> 00:04:41,580
could wipe out their entire
communications network.

80
00:04:41,860 --> 00:04:47,367
In 1959, an engineer named Paul Baran
joined the RAND Corporation to solve

81
00:04:47,367 --> 00:04:49,228
this exact vulnerability.

82
00:04:49,508 --> 00:04:53,828
He knew that the traditional telephone
network relied on dedicated circuits.

83
00:04:54,108 --> 00:04:58,116
If a main line or station was destroyed,
the connection went dead.

84
00:04:58,396 --> 00:05:02,884
Baran set out to design a system that
could survive a devastating attack.

85
00:05:03,164 --> 00:05:07,388
He began running computer simulations to
test how networks would hold up.

86
00:05:07,668 --> 00:05:11,484
He set up models where nodes had
different numbers of connection links.

87
00:05:11,764 --> 00:05:16,042
In these digital tests, he randomly
destroyed different nodes to see what

88
00:05:16,042 --> 00:05:16,804
would happen.

89
00:05:17,084 --> 00:05:19,988
The simulations revealed a striking
pattern.

90
00:05:20,268 --> 00:05:24,300
If each node was connected to at least
three others, the network could

91
00:05:24,300 --> 00:05:28,217
survive a massive fifty percent loss of
its nodes and still keep the

92
00:05:28,217 --> 00:05:29,772
remaining points connected.

93
00:05:30,052 --> 00:05:32,908
Redundancy was the secret to survival.

94
00:05:33,188 --> 00:05:37,196
To make this work, Baran proposed a
radical new architecture.

95
00:05:37,476 --> 00:05:41,907
Instead of keeping a dedicated line open
for a whole conversation, he

96
00:05:41,907 --> 00:05:46,338
suggested breaking messages into small,
uniform pieces called message

97
00:05:46,338 --> 00:05:46,788
blocks.

98
00:05:47,068 --> 00:05:49,204
Each block would be sent out separately.

99
00:05:49,484 --> 00:05:54,113
They would travel independently, finding
their own paths through a web of

100
00:05:54,113 --> 00:05:55,508
cheap, low-cost nodes.

101
00:05:55,788 --> 00:06:00,356
If a path was blocked or destroyed, the
blocks would simply route around the

102
00:06:00,356 --> 00:06:03,060
damage and rejoin at their final
destination.

103
00:06:03,340 --> 00:06:08,038
This design meant the network did not
need expensive, perfect equipment to

104
00:06:08,038 --> 00:06:09,244
be highly reliable.

105
00:06:09,524 --> 00:06:13,370
But when Baran presented this concept to
the telecommunications

106
00:06:13,370 --> 00:06:15,812
establishment, he met fierce resistance.

107
00:06:16,092 --> 00:06:20,508
Traditional telephone engineers at AT&T
scoffed at his ideas.

108
00:06:20,788 --> 00:06:23,620
They were deeply invested in dedicated
circuits.

109
00:06:23,900 --> 00:06:28,289
They insisted that his system of non-
dedicated physical paths could never

110
00:06:28,289 --> 00:06:29,492
support voice calls.

111
00:06:29,772 --> 00:06:34,305
Some even told Baran to his face that he
simply did not understand how voice

112
00:06:34,305 --> 00:06:35,796
telecommunication worked.

113
00:06:36,076 --> 00:06:41,015
This military research, born from the
fear of nuclear war, seemed dead in

114
00:06:41,015 --> 00:06:41,692
the water.

115
00:06:41,972 --> 00:06:46,687
Yet, it laid the exact groundwork for
the myth that would forever change how

116
00:06:46,687 --> 00:06:48,548
we view the origin of the web.

117
00:06:49,448 --> 00:06:54,051
Paul Baran's ideas about surviving a
nuclear strike were ignored in America,

118
00:06:54,051 --> 00:06:58,472
but a completely different spark was
about to ignite across the Atlantic.

119
00:06:58,752 --> 00:07:03,072
This new breakthrough came not from
military planners, but from a civilian

120
00:07:03,072 --> 00:07:05,640
scientist focused on the future of
business.

121
00:07:05,920 --> 00:07:10,846
In 1965, a British computer scientist
named Donald Davies was working at the

122
00:07:10,846 --> 00:07:14,152
National Physical Laboratory in the
United Kingdom.

123
00:07:14,432 --> 00:07:17,480
Davies was no stranger to cutting-edge
technology.

124
00:07:17,760 --> 00:07:22,223
He had previously worked alongside Alan
Turing, helping to build some of the

125
00:07:22,223 --> 00:07:23,808
world's earliest computers.

126
00:07:24,088 --> 00:07:29,214
His interest in communication networks
grew after a trip to New York and MIT

127
00:07:29,214 --> 00:07:33,868
in 1965, where he discussed the
engineering struggles of time-sharing

128
00:07:33,868 --> 00:07:34,408
systems.

129
00:07:34,688 --> 00:07:38,863
Davies realized that computer traffic
was completely different from voice

130
00:07:38,863 --> 00:07:39,320
traffic.

131
00:07:39,600 --> 00:07:44,352
When humans talk on the phone, the
stream of sound is relatively constant.

132
00:07:44,632 --> 00:07:47,368
But computer traffic is inherently
bursty.

133
00:07:47,648 --> 00:07:52,178
You have quick, sudden bursts of
activity followed by long stretches of

134
00:07:52,178 --> 00:07:52,688
silence.

135
00:07:52,968 --> 00:07:57,343
Keeping a dedicated telephone circuit
open during these silent gaps was

136
00:07:57,343 --> 00:07:59,376
highly inefficient and expensive.

137
00:07:59,656 --> 00:08:03,967
It was like keeping a water tap running
at full blast just to catch a single

138
00:08:03,967 --> 00:08:05,272
drop every few minutes.

139
00:08:05,552 --> 00:08:10,255
To solve this, Davies proposed breaking
computer messages into very short,

140
00:08:10,255 --> 00:08:13,496
fixed-size pieces that could be sent
independently.

141
00:08:13,776 --> 00:08:15,984
He wanted a term that would work
globally.

142
00:08:16,264 --> 00:08:19,816
After consulting with a linguist, he
coined the term packet.

143
00:08:20,096 --> 00:08:24,248
He chose this word because it could
easily translate into other languages

144
00:08:24,248 --> 00:08:25,784
without losing its meaning.

145
00:08:26,064 --> 00:08:31,115
In 1966, Davies published a proposal for
a commercial national data network

146
00:08:31,115 --> 00:08:32,664
for the United Kingdom.

147
00:08:32,944 --> 00:08:37,336
In this paper, he introduced the concept
of high-speed switching nodes,

148
00:08:37,336 --> 00:08:38,944
which we now call routers.

149
00:08:39,224 --> 00:08:43,855
He even used mathematical queueing
theory to prove that these packets would

150
00:08:43,855 --> 00:08:46,448
travel fast enough to satisfy human
users.

151
00:08:46,728 --> 00:08:51,619
Davies predicted that one day, a single
network would carry both speech and

152
00:08:51,619 --> 00:08:52,728
digital messages.

153
00:08:53,008 --> 00:08:56,776
He was not trying to build a military
shield against a nuclear attack.

154
00:08:57,056 --> 00:09:00,935
Instead, he was trying to make computer
time-sharing cheaper and more

155
00:09:00,935 --> 00:09:03,464
efficient for everyday business and
shopping.

156
00:09:03,744 --> 00:09:08,548
Yet, this peaceful British project would
soon cross paths with the American

157
00:09:08,548 --> 00:09:12,648
military network, forever changing the
blueprint of the ARPANET.

158
00:09:13,548 --> 00:09:17,928
Donald Davies was designing a system for
commerce, but back in the United

159
00:09:17,928 --> 00:09:22,428
States, Bob Taylor was about to secure
the massive financial backing needed

160
00:09:22,428 --> 00:09:25,788
to turn these networking concepts into
physical reality.

161
00:09:26,068 --> 00:09:28,588
Taylor did not just want to talk about
ideas.

162
00:09:28,868 --> 00:09:30,740
He wanted to build something real.

163
00:09:31,020 --> 00:09:37,127
In February 1966, Taylor successfully
lobbied the director of ARPA, Charles

164
00:09:37,127 --> 00:09:37,860
Herzfeld.

165
00:09:38,140 --> 00:09:42,322
He laid out his vision of a cooperative
network of remote computers to

166
00:09:42,322 --> 00:09:43,756
enable resource sharing.

167
00:09:44,036 --> 00:09:48,515
This would allow remote researchers at
different academic and corporate

168
00:09:48,515 --> 00:09:52,364
locations to easily utilize computers
provided by the agency.

169
00:09:52,644 --> 00:09:55,452
Herzfeld listened intently to the
proposal.

170
00:09:55,732 --> 00:09:58,444
Within minutes, the director was
convinced.

171
00:09:58,724 --> 00:10:03,320
He agreed to support the project and
instantly authorized a massive budget

172
00:10:03,320 --> 00:10:04,748
of one million dollars.

173
00:10:05,028 --> 00:10:07,500
Where did this huge sum of cash come
from?

174
00:10:07,780 --> 00:10:12,405
Herzfeld did not wait for congressional
approvals or slow bureaucratic

175
00:10:12,405 --> 00:10:12,868
cycles.

176
00:10:13,148 --> 00:10:17,142
Instead, he took the money directly from
a ballistic missile defense

177
00:10:17,142 --> 00:10:17,612
program.

178
00:10:17,892 --> 00:10:21,060
He simply redirected those funds to
Taylor's budget.

179
00:10:21,340 --> 00:10:25,666
This bold financial maneuver meant the
network was funded using money

180
00:10:25,666 --> 00:10:27,484
originally meant for weapons.

181
00:10:27,764 --> 00:10:31,864
It was a clean transfer of resources
from defense shields to digital

182
00:10:31,864 --> 00:10:32,588
connections.

183
00:10:32,868 --> 00:10:37,258
With the cash secured, Taylor needed
someone to actually manage the project

184
00:10:37,258 --> 00:10:38,604
and build this network.

185
00:10:38,884 --> 00:10:43,626
He wanted a program manager to work on
the ARPANET in the ARPA Information

186
00:10:43,626 --> 00:10:45,484
Processing Techniques Office.

187
00:10:45,764 --> 00:10:48,476
He chose Larry Roberts for this
important role.

188
00:10:48,756 --> 00:10:54,252
In January 1967, Roberts officially
joined the office to lead the program.

189
00:10:54,532 --> 00:10:59,168
His task was to take these theoretical
ideas and turn them into a practical,

190
00:10:59,168 --> 00:11:00,388
functioning network.

191
00:11:00,668 --> 00:11:03,764
Roberts immediately began gathering
information.

192
00:11:04,044 --> 00:11:08,652
Shortly after taking the job, he met the
famous RAND researcher Paul Baran.

193
00:11:08,932 --> 00:11:13,125
You might expect that this meeting of
minds would spark a deep, historic

194
00:11:13,125 --> 00:11:16,036
debate about the future of digital
communications.

195
00:11:16,316 --> 00:11:18,188
But it did not.

196
00:11:18,468 --> 00:11:22,452
During their initial meeting, they did
not discuss computer networks at all.

197
00:11:22,732 --> 00:11:26,116
Their conversation focused on entirely
different topics.

198
00:11:26,396 --> 00:11:30,568
The various elements of the network were
present, but they had not yet been

199
00:11:30,568 --> 00:11:31,124
connected.

200
00:11:31,404 --> 00:11:35,771
The true integration of their ideas was
still to come, and the blueprint of

201
00:11:35,771 --> 00:11:38,508
the network was about to undergo a major
shift.

202
00:11:39,408 --> 00:11:43,617
Roberts had to figure out how to
physically link these massive university

203
00:11:43,617 --> 00:11:44,712
computers together.

204
00:11:44,992 --> 00:11:46,864
His first idea was simple.

205
00:11:47,144 --> 00:11:51,588
He proposed that every large mainframe
computer on the network would connect

206
00:11:51,588 --> 00:11:53,576
directly to every other mainframe.

207
00:11:53,856 --> 00:11:58,697
But this plan immediately ran into a
wall of resistance from the university

208
00:11:58,697 --> 00:11:59,472
researchers.

209
00:11:59,752 --> 00:12:03,688
These scientists did not want to waste
their precious computing power on

210
00:12:03,688 --> 00:12:04,672
running a network.

211
00:12:04,952 --> 00:12:08,912
They wanted their expensive machines to
focus on calculations, not

212
00:12:08,912 --> 00:12:10,232
administrative chores.

213
00:12:10,512 --> 00:12:14,458
They felt that managing data traffic was
a distraction from their real

214
00:12:14,458 --> 00:12:15,360
scientific work.

215
00:12:15,640 --> 00:12:19,883
That was when an engineer named Wesley
Clark stepped in with a clever

216
00:12:19,883 --> 00:12:20,560
workaround.

217
00:12:20,840 --> 00:12:25,258
He suggested putting a smaller,
dedicated minicomputer in front of each

218
00:12:25,258 --> 00:12:25,880
mainframe.

219
00:12:26,160 --> 00:12:30,577
This intermediary machine would act as a
translator, handling all the messy

220
00:12:30,577 --> 00:12:34,877
network traffic and routing messages so
the mainframes did not have to do

221
00:12:34,877 --> 00:12:35,760
the extra work.

222
00:12:36,040 --> 00:12:37,912
Roberts loved the idea.

223
00:12:38,192 --> 00:12:43,363
He modified his plans and called these
specialized minicomputers Interface

224
00:12:43,363 --> 00:12:45,320
Message Processors, or IMPs.

225
00:12:45,600 --> 00:12:48,456
But how would these IMPs actually send
data?

226
00:12:48,736 --> 00:12:53,689
The answer came during a trip to
Tennessee for a major computer
conference

227
00:12:53,689 --> 00:12:54,760
in October 1967.

228
00:12:55,040 --> 00:12:59,887
There, a British scientist named Roger
Scantlebury stood before the ARPA

229
00:12:59,887 --> 00:13:00,224
team.

230
00:13:00,504 --> 00:13:04,296
He described Donald Davies' work on
packet switching in England.

231
00:13:04,576 --> 00:13:09,444
Scantlebury explained that breaking data
into small, uniform pieces was far

232
00:13:09,444 --> 00:13:12,040
more efficient than traditional methods.

233
00:13:12,320 --> 00:13:16,255
He even pointed out that the Americans
were lagging behind the British in

234
00:13:16,255 --> 00:13:18,680
this field, which caught Roberts by
surprise.

235
00:13:18,960 --> 00:13:22,224
This presentation changed everything for
Roberts.

236
00:13:22,504 --> 00:13:26,440
He realized that packet switching was
the missing piece of his puzzle.

237
00:13:26,720 --> 00:13:31,808
He adopted Davies' exact packet size of
1024 bits for the new network.

238
00:13:32,088 --> 00:13:36,220
He also went back to review Paul Baran's
RAND Corporation research,

239
00:13:36,220 --> 00:13:40,845
incorporating those ideas on distributed
networks to make the system highly

240
00:13:40,845 --> 00:13:41,400
reliable.

241
00:13:41,680 --> 00:13:43,912
The blueprint had completely changed.

242
00:13:44,192 --> 00:13:49,003
Instead of a direct web of giant
mainframes, the ARPANET would now be a

243
00:13:49,003 --> 00:13:53,610
network of smart minicomputers talking
to each other in standardized

244
00:13:53,610 --> 00:13:54,152
packets.

245
00:13:54,432 --> 00:13:58,825
The theoretical ideas had finally fused
into a concrete plan, and now,

246
00:13:58,825 --> 00:14:02,088
someone had to actually build the
physical hardware.

247
00:14:02,988 --> 00:14:07,475
With a solid plan for these specialized
interface computers on paper, the

248
00:14:07,475 --> 00:14:09,012
race to build them began.

249
00:14:09,292 --> 00:14:14,761
In 1969, ARPA officially awarded the
contract to construct these Interface

250
00:14:14,761 --> 00:14:20,231
Message Processors to a Massachusetts-
based company named Bolt Beranek and

251
00:14:20,231 --> 00:14:22,300
Newman, widely known as BBN.

252
00:14:22,580 --> 00:14:26,965
An engineer named Frank Heart took the
reins of this project, leading a

253
00:14:26,965 --> 00:14:31,473
highly dedicated group of engineers and
programmers who called themselves

254
00:14:31,473 --> 00:14:32,276
the IMP Guys.

255
00:14:32,556 --> 00:14:37,253
Their task was to take rugged Honeywell
minicomputers, designed to withstand

256
00:14:37,253 --> 00:14:41,950
harsh physical environments, and turn
them into the world's earliest network

257
00:14:41,950 --> 00:14:42,444
routers.

258
00:14:42,724 --> 00:14:47,338
They worked tirelessly in their offices,
writing the complex software in a

259
00:14:47,338 --> 00:14:49,084
low-level assembly language.

260
00:14:49,364 --> 00:14:53,601
This was a tedious process of writing
instructions that spoke directly to

261
00:14:53,601 --> 00:14:55,748
the computer's raw hardware circuits.

262
00:14:56,028 --> 00:15:00,180
But getting these machines to talk to
the massive university mainframes

263
00:15:00,180 --> 00:15:02,052
required a precise set of rules.

264
00:15:02,332 --> 00:15:07,315
Bob Kahn, a key member of the team, took
on the challenge of designing the

265
00:15:07,315 --> 00:15:09,268
initial host-to-IMP protocol.

266
00:15:09,548 --> 00:15:13,890
A protocol is simply a standardized
digital handshake, a shared language

267
00:15:13,890 --> 00:15:18,473
that allows completely different kinds
of computers to exchange data without

268
00:15:18,473 --> 00:15:19,076
confusion.

269
00:15:19,356 --> 00:15:25,448
Kahn documented these exact rules in a
technical specification that became

270
00:15:25,448 --> 00:15:27,588
famous as BBN Report 1822.

271
00:15:27,868 --> 00:15:32,303
This document became the bible for any
university that wanted to plug its

272
00:15:32,303 --> 00:15:34,612
massive computer into the new network.

273
00:15:34,892 --> 00:15:39,361
The BBN team also had to make sure that
data would not get scrambled during

274
00:15:39,361 --> 00:15:40,076
its journey.

275
00:15:40,356 --> 00:15:45,207
To solve this, the IMP hardware was
designed to automatically calculate a

276
00:15:45,207 --> 00:15:48,396
24-bit checksum for every single packet
of data.

277
00:15:48,676 --> 00:15:51,844
This checksum acted like a quick
mathematical lock.

278
00:15:52,124 --> 00:15:56,373
If a packet arrived with its numbers
scrambled, the lock would not fit, and

279
00:15:56,373 --> 00:16:00,623
the receiving machine would instantly
know the data was corrupted and throw

280
00:16:00,623 --> 00:16:01,076
it away.

281
00:16:01,356 --> 00:16:06,010
This automatic hardware check was
incredibly fast, allowing the network to

282
00:16:06,010 --> 00:16:09,972
detect and discard bad packets without
slowing down the system.

283
00:16:10,252 --> 00:16:14,380
The source machine would then simply
send a clean copy of the packet again.

284
00:16:14,660 --> 00:16:19,394
Through this combination of rugged
hardware, custom assembly code, and

285
00:16:19,394 --> 00:16:23,722
strict protocols, the BBN team
successfully transformed abstract

286
00:16:23,722 --> 00:16:27,644
packet-switching theories into physical,
working machines.

287
00:16:27,924 --> 00:16:31,485
They were finally ready to ship these
digital messengers to the

288
00:16:31,485 --> 00:16:32,220
universities.

289
00:16:33,120 --> 00:16:37,602
These newly completed machines were
finally ready to leave the laboratory

290
00:16:37,602 --> 00:16:40,488
and face their ultimate test in the real
world.

291
00:16:40,768 --> 00:16:46,515
The very first interface computer
arrived at the University of California,

292
00:16:46,515 --> 00:16:49,000
Los Angeles, on August 30, 1969.

293
00:16:49,280 --> 00:16:53,307
It was delivered to a measurement group
led by Leonard Kleinrock, who was

294
00:16:53,307 --> 00:16:57,224
tasked with analyzing how this new
network would behave under pressure.

295
00:16:57,504 --> 00:17:02,069
This heavy, metal machine was about the
size of a household refrigerator,

296
00:17:02,069 --> 00:17:06,696
yet it carried the entire future of
digital communication on its circuits.

297
00:17:06,976 --> 00:17:12,203
Just over a month later, on October 1,
1969, the second interface computer

298
00:17:12,203 --> 00:17:15,664
was installed at the Stanford Research
Institute.

299
00:17:15,944 --> 00:17:20,298
This second machine was hooked up to a
host computer in a research group run

300
00:17:20,298 --> 00:17:24,480
by Douglas Engelbart, the visionary who
had already invented the computer

301
00:17:24,480 --> 00:17:24,824
mouse.

302
00:17:25,104 --> 00:17:30,279
Now, two distant nodes were finally in
place, separated by hundreds of miles

303
00:17:30,279 --> 00:17:31,368
of open country.

304
00:17:31,648 --> 00:17:34,192
The stage was set for a historic
handshake.

305
00:17:34,472 --> 00:17:40,919
On the evening of October 29, 1969, a
young UCLA student named Charley Kline

306
00:17:40,919 --> 00:17:43,040
sat down to make history.

307
00:17:43,320 --> 00:17:47,684
His goal was simple: connect the UCLA
computer to the Stanford machine,

308
00:17:47,684 --> 00:17:50,880
allowing him to type commands from his
own keyboard.

309
00:17:51,160 --> 00:17:54,917
He sat in a quiet room, wearing a
telephone headset to speak with a

310
00:17:54,917 --> 00:17:56,656
researcher at the Stanford lab.

311
00:17:56,936 --> 00:18:00,752
To establish the connection, Kline
needed to type the word login.

312
00:18:01,032 --> 00:18:03,072
He carefully pressed the letter l.

313
00:18:03,352 --> 00:18:07,587
Over the headset, his colleague
confirmed that the Stanford machine
received

314
00:18:07,587 --> 00:18:08,200
the letter.

315
00:18:08,480 --> 00:18:10,784
Next, Kline pressed the letter o.

316
00:18:11,064 --> 00:18:15,360
Once again, the voice on the phone
confirmed the letter arrived safely.

317
00:18:15,640 --> 00:18:17,968
Then, Kline pressed the letter g.

318
00:18:18,248 --> 00:18:20,600
Suddenly, the connection went dead.

319
00:18:20,880 --> 00:18:24,816
The Stanford computer had crashed upon
receiving that fourth character.

320
00:18:25,096 --> 00:18:29,313
The system was overwhelmed by this
brand-new way of talking, causing an

321
00:18:29,313 --> 00:18:30,976
immediate technical failure.

322
00:18:31,256 --> 00:18:35,120
But those first two letters had already
traveled across the wires.

323
00:18:35,400 --> 00:18:39,840
By crashing at that exact moment, the
system left an accidental message.

324
00:18:40,120 --> 00:18:44,359
The word lo became the very first
transmission in the history of the

325
00:18:44,359 --> 00:18:44,920
internet.

326
00:18:45,200 --> 00:18:49,802
It was a humble, almost poetic
beginning, sounding like an old English

327
00:18:49,802 --> 00:18:51,248
greeting of discovery.

328
00:18:51,528 --> 00:18:55,985
Within a short time, the engineers found
the bug, fixed the software, and

329
00:18:55,985 --> 00:18:57,816
completed the full connection.

330
00:18:58,096 --> 00:19:02,869
The network was alive, but this dramatic
first attempt proved that building

331
00:19:02,869 --> 00:19:07,451
a global web would be a journey of
constant trial, error, and unexpected

332
00:19:07,451 --> 00:19:07,960
failure.

333
00:19:08,860 --> 00:19:12,749
Those early wires on the mainland
connected static locations, but a

334
00:19:12,749 --> 00:19:16,811
different team of engineers wanted to
free computer data from physical

335
00:19:16,811 --> 00:19:17,740
cables entirely.

336
00:19:18,020 --> 00:19:23,213
In September 1968, Norman Abramson and
Franklin Kuo began developing a

337
00:19:23,213 --> 00:19:26,996
system called ALOHAnet at the University
of Hawaii.

338
00:19:27,276 --> 00:19:30,156
They faced a unique geographical
challenge.

339
00:19:30,436 --> 00:19:35,022
Hawaii is made of separate islands, and
running physical cables underwater

340
00:19:35,022 --> 00:19:39,484
to connect every campus computer was
incredibly difficult and expensive.

341
00:19:39,764 --> 00:19:43,580
They decided to use low-cost commercial
radio equipment instead.

342
00:19:43,860 --> 00:19:48,264
Their goal was to link users across the
islands with a central time-sharing

343
00:19:48,264 --> 00:19:49,908
computer on the main campus.

344
00:19:50,188 --> 00:19:55,354
Their system became operational in June
1971, marking the very first public

345
00:19:55,354 --> 00:19:58,660
demonstration of a wireless packet data
network.

346
00:19:58,940 --> 00:20:02,909
How did these remote computers talk to
each other without wires getting

347
00:20:02,909 --> 00:20:03,356
crossed?

348
00:20:03,636 --> 00:20:07,524
The system used a method called Pure
ALOHA random access.

349
00:20:07,804 --> 00:20:12,502
In this setup, when a computer had data
to send, it simply transmitted the

350
00:20:12,502 --> 00:20:13,708
packet immediately.

351
00:20:13,988 --> 00:20:16,844
It did not check to see if the radio
channel was clear.

352
00:20:17,124 --> 00:20:20,052
It did not wait its turn or ask for
permission.

353
00:20:20,332 --> 00:20:23,476
The node just broadcasted its message
into the air.

354
00:20:23,756 --> 00:20:27,463
It was like a room full of people where
everyone shouts their message

355
00:20:27,463 --> 00:20:28,484
whenever they want.

356
00:20:28,764 --> 00:20:33,826
If two people speak at once, they both
stop, wait a random moment, and try

357
00:20:33,826 --> 00:20:34,236
again.

358
00:20:34,516 --> 00:20:38,832
Of course, this meant two computers
would sometimes talk at the exact same

359
00:20:38,832 --> 00:20:39,124
time.

360
00:20:39,404 --> 00:20:43,556
When this happened, the radio signals
collided and destroyed the data.

361
00:20:43,836 --> 00:20:47,922
To solve this, the sending computer
waited for a quick receipt confirmation

362
00:20:47,922 --> 00:20:49,284
from the central station.

363
00:20:49,564 --> 00:20:53,583
If that confirmation did not arrive, the
computer knew a collision had

364
00:20:53,583 --> 00:20:54,100
occurred.

365
00:20:54,380 --> 00:20:58,688
It would simply wait for a randomly
chosen split second and try sending the

366
00:20:58,688 --> 00:20:59,492
message again.

367
00:20:59,772 --> 00:21:03,948
This simple, chaotic shared-medium
protocol worked surprisingly well.

368
00:21:04,228 --> 00:21:08,678
It solved the massive problem of
coordinating multiple devices on a
single

369
00:21:08,678 --> 00:21:09,580
shared channel.

370
00:21:09,860 --> 00:21:14,464
Decades later, this exact random-access
concept laid the direct technical

371
00:21:14,464 --> 00:21:19,130
groundwork for the modern Ethernet
cables plugged into our offices and the

372
00:21:19,130 --> 00:21:21,716
Wi-Fi networks we use in our homes
today.

373
00:21:21,996 --> 00:21:24,948
Packet switching had successfully taken
flight.

374
00:21:25,848 --> 00:21:30,027
Wireless signals were flying through the
Hawaiian air, and cables were

375
00:21:30,027 --> 00:21:34,385
humming across the American mainland,
but these early networks were still

376
00:21:34,385 --> 00:21:35,400
isolated islands.

377
00:21:35,680 --> 00:21:40,465
An ARPANET computer could not easily
talk to a radio-based system like

378
00:21:40,465 --> 00:21:41,080
ALOHAnet.

379
00:21:41,360 --> 00:21:45,800
Each network used its own unique
language, rules, and hardware.

380
00:21:46,080 --> 00:21:51,640
To solve this digital tower of Babel,
two scientists stepped forward in

381
00:21:51,640 --> 00:21:52,032
1973.

382
00:21:52,312 --> 00:21:57,106
Bob Kahn and Vint Cerf set out to design
a system that could join completely

383
00:21:57,106 --> 00:21:59,944
separate networks into a single, unified
web.

384
00:22:00,224 --> 00:22:03,464
They called their solution the
Transmission Control Program.

385
00:22:03,744 --> 00:22:07,693
They wanted a cooperative setup where no
single computer controlled the

386
00:22:07,693 --> 00:22:10,752
whole system, and no single failure
could shut it down.

387
00:22:11,032 --> 00:22:15,827
To make this work, they eventually split
the massive job into two distinct

388
00:22:15,827 --> 00:22:16,216
roles.

389
00:22:16,496 --> 00:22:20,960
First, they created the Transmission
Control Protocol, or TCP.

390
00:22:21,240 --> 00:22:24,456
This part of the system acted like a
careful inspector.

391
00:22:24,736 --> 00:22:29,252
It broke data down, kept track of the
pieces, and made sure they arrived in

392
00:22:29,252 --> 00:22:31,480
the correct order without any errors.

393
00:22:31,760 --> 00:22:35,552
Second, they created the Internet
Protocol, or IP.

394
00:22:35,832 --> 00:22:40,233
This was the navigator, responsible for
addressing and routing the data to

395
00:22:40,233 --> 00:22:41,184
its destination.

396
00:22:41,464 --> 00:22:44,056
Kahn and Cerf did not work in a vacuum.

397
00:22:44,336 --> 00:22:48,603
They gathered some of their best ideas
from other creative minds around the

398
00:22:48,603 --> 00:22:48,944
world.

399
00:22:49,224 --> 00:22:53,147
They incorporated concepts from Louis
Pouzin's French network project,

400
00:22:53,147 --> 00:22:57,294
called CYCLADES, which proved that
computers at the ends of the connection

401
00:22:57,294 --> 00:22:59,424
could handle their own error checking.

402
00:22:59,704 --> 00:23:03,304
They also drew from Bob Metcalfe's work
at Xerox PARC.

403
00:23:03,584 --> 00:23:08,397
By combining these international ideas,
they built a universal translator

404
00:23:08,397 --> 00:23:09,320
for computers.

405
00:23:09,600 --> 00:23:14,086
It did not matter if a machine was
hooked to a telephone wire, a radio

406
00:23:14,086 --> 00:23:15,816
tower, or a satellite dish.

407
00:23:16,096 --> 00:23:20,367
As long as it spoke this new shared
language, it could join the global

408
00:23:20,367 --> 00:23:21,160
conversation.

409
00:23:21,440 --> 00:23:25,592
The big transition happened on January
1, 1983.

410
00:23:25,872 --> 00:23:30,822
On that day, version four of the TCP and
IP suite became the official

411
00:23:30,822 --> 00:23:32,616
standard for the ARPANET.

412
00:23:32,896 --> 00:23:37,297
Every computer on the network had to
switch over to the new protocols at the

413
00:23:37,297 --> 00:23:38,224
exact same time.

414
00:23:38,504 --> 00:23:42,785
This historic switch established the
modern technical bedrock of the

415
00:23:42,785 --> 00:23:43,352
Internet.

416
00:23:43,632 --> 00:23:48,152
The isolated networks were gone,
replaced by a true network of networks
that

417
00:23:48,152 --> 00:23:50,352
could grow to cover the entire globe.

418
00:23:51,252 --> 00:23:55,404
So, did the internet really start as a
shield against nuclear war?

419
00:23:55,684 --> 00:23:58,180
The answer is a fascinating middle
ground.

420
00:23:58,460 --> 00:24:02,348
ARPANET itself was not built to survive
a nuclear conflict.

421
00:24:02,628 --> 00:24:07,030
Instead, its creators designed it for a
much more practical purpose: to

422
00:24:07,030 --> 00:24:10,812
share expensive computer resources among
distant researchers.

423
00:24:11,092 --> 00:24:14,284
Yet, the famous myth holds a kernel of
truth.

424
00:24:14,564 --> 00:24:18,891
The network succeeded because it adopted
packet-switching technology, a

425
00:24:18,891 --> 00:24:23,279
concept born directly from Paul Baran’s
military research into surviving

426
00:24:23,279 --> 00:24:25,412
atomic attacks during the Cold War.

427
00:24:25,692 --> 00:24:29,484
As the years passed, newer networks
expanded this reach.

428
00:24:29,764 --> 00:24:35,126
The National Science Foundation funded
the NSFNET in 1986, which connected

429
00:24:35,126 --> 00:24:37,444
universities across the country.

430
00:24:37,724 --> 00:24:40,844
This paved the way for massive
commercial expansion.

431
00:24:41,124 --> 00:24:46,591
Finally, in 1990, the ARPANET was
formally decommissioned, leaving a
global

432
00:24:46,591 --> 00:24:48,996
network of networks in its place.

433
00:24:49,276 --> 00:24:53,666
The ultimate takeaway is that the
resilience of your digital world is a

434
00:24:53,666 --> 00:24:54,532
hybrid legacy.

435
00:24:54,812 --> 00:24:59,716
It is a unique combination of Cold War
military planning and open academic

436
00:24:59,716 --> 00:25:00,644
collaboration.

437
00:25:00,924 --> 00:25:05,663
This blend of survival-focused
engineering and cooperative resource-
sharing

438
00:25:05,663 --> 00:25:09,012
is exactly what keeps our global network
alive today.

439
00:25:09,292 --> 00:25:13,708
Without both forces, the modern internet
as we know it would not exist.

440
00:25:13,988 --> 00:25:16,196
Thanks for exploring this history with
me.
